    <article class="doc">
      <div class="doc-top"><p class="eyebrow">Get started</p><div class="page-actions"><button type="button" class="ghost-btn" data-copy-page="/changelog.md"><svg class="icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="14" height="14" x="8" y="8" rx="2" ry="2"/> <path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2"/></svg><span>Copy page</span></button><a class="ghost-btn" href="/changelog.md"><svg class="icon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 22a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h8a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 1.704.706l3.588 3.588A2.4 2.4 0 0 1 20 8v12a2 2 0 0 1-2 2z"/> <path d="M14 2v5a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h5"/> <path d="M10 9H8"/> <path d="M16 13H8"/> <path d="M16 17H8"/></svg><span>Markdown</span></a></div></div>
      <h1>Changelog</h1>
<p>Changes that affect what an integration sends, reads or shows, newest first. The machine-readable contract is always the live <a href="/openapi.json">OpenAPI document</a>; this page explains what changed and what to do about it.</p>
<h2 id="2026-11-22-hxmr-replaces-xmr1-on-the-bridge"><a class="anchor" href="#2026-11-22-hxmr-replaces-xmr1-on-the-bridge" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>2026-11-22: HXMR replaces XMR1 on the bridge</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>HXMR</strong> is the wrapped Monero the native bridge now issues and accepts on Hyperliquid: token index <code>1339</code>, token ID <code>0x700beac50c950503f401d218e52014e7</code>, spot market <code>@1193</code> (HXMR/USDC). The full identity is on <a href="/native-bridge#hxmr-token-identity">the bridge page</a>.</li>
<li>The bridge asset key is still <code>xmr</code>. Create deposits and withdrawals exactly as before: <code>POST /v2/bridges</code> with <code>&quot;asset&quot;: &quot;xmr&quot;</code>.</li>
<li>A withdrawal waiting for HXMR has the new status <strong><code>awaiting_hxmr</code></strong>. Add it wherever you handle order statuses; until you do, treat any unknown status as not final and keep tracking the order.</li>
<li>Bridge amounts stay in XMR atomic units (12 decimals) on both sides, including HXMR sent to a withdrawal.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="names-kept-on-purpose"><a class="anchor" href="#names-kept-on-purpose" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>Names kept on purpose</h3>
<p>So that existing clients keep working, some names still say XMR1:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Name</th>
<th>What it carries now</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><code>xmr1DepositAddress</code> on a withdrawal order</td>
<td>The Hyperliquid address to send <strong>HXMR</strong> to.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>awaiting_xmr1</code>, <code>awaiting_zec1</code>, <code>awaiting_trx1</code></td>
<td>Legacy statuses of orders created before each token closed. No new order gets them. See <a href="/order-lifecycle#legacy-statuses">legacy statuses</a>.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="2026-11-21-zec1-and-zcash-closed"><a class="anchor" href="#2026-11-21-zec1-and-zcash-closed" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>2026-11-21: ZEC1 and Zcash closed</h2>
<ul>
<li>ZEC1 (token <code>1021</code>, market <code>@867</code>) was closed and has no successor. The <code>zec</code> bridge asset is switched off for good, and every swap from or to Zcash is closed: Zcash is no longer in the chain catalog.</li>
<li>Orders that were still open in ZEC1 are settled or refunded by support.</li>
<li>Remove Zcash from your pickers. Do not quote or create Zcash routes.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="2026-11-20-xmr1-closed"><a class="anchor" href="#2026-11-20-xmr1-closed" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>2026-11-20: XMR1 closed</h2>
<ul>
<li>XMR1 (token <code>404</code>, market <code>@260</code>) was closed after its deployer key was compromised. It is <strong>no longer a claim on Wagyu&#39;s Monero</strong> and the bridge no longer accepts it.</li>
<li>Orders that were open in XMR1 are settled or refunded by support. Show them as waiting on support; never ask a user to send XMR1.</li>
<li>Do not list, price or accept XMR1 as wrapped XMR. Use HXMR.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="2026-11-11-trx1-bridge-retired"><a class="anchor" href="#2026-11-11-trx1-bridge-retired" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>2026-11-11: TRX1 bridge retired</h2>
<ul>
<li>The Tron native bridge (TRX1) was retired; the <code>trx</code> bridge asset is switched off for good.</li>
<li>Tron itself is unaffected: it remains a swap chain, listed in <a href="/chains">Chains and tokens</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="new-in-the-contract-version-0-8-0"><a class="anchor" href="#new-in-the-contract-version-0-8-0" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a>New in the contract (version 0.8.0)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>paused</code> error code</strong> (<code>409</code>): Wagyu switched the requested pair, asset, bridge direction or product off. The message names what is paused. It replaces the <code>503 unavailable</code> answer for a switched-off bridge asset or direction; <code>unavailable</code> now means only a temporary failure on Wagyu&#39;s side. Do not retry a <code>paused</code> answer in a loop. See <a href="/actions-and-retries#error-codes">error codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong><code>GET /v2/notice</code></strong> returns the current site-wide notice (<code>info</code>, <code>warning</code> or <code>outage</code>), or <code>null</code>. See <a href="/public-data#site-notice">Public data</a>.</li>
<li><strong><code>awaiting_hxmr</code></strong>, as above.</li>
<li><strong><code>trackerUrl</code></strong> on each leg of a swap order&#39;s <code>route</code>: a link to follow that leg on its route provider&#39;s public tracker, or <code>null</code> when there is none.</li>
</ul>

